<h1>bucket</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
An area of storage where items with a common property are stored. Typically <a href="tree.html"><em>tree</em></a> data structures and <a href="sort.html"><em>sort</em></a> algorithms use many buckets, one for each group of items.  Usually buckets are kept on disk.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="bag.html"><em>bag</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="radixsort.html"><em>radix sort</em></a>, <a href="bucketsort.html"><em>bucket sort</em></a>, <a href="elasticBucketTrie.html"><em>elastic-bucket trie</em></a>, <a href="hashheap.html"><em>hash heap</em></a>, <a href="extendibleHashing.html"><em>extendible hashing</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A <em>bucket</em> is used when a number of items need to be kept together, but the order among them is not important. Conceptually it is a <a href="bag.html"><em>bag</em></a> (rather than a <a href="set.html"><em>set</em></a>).</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

